<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Canton System</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1757">1757</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> System was the arrangement formalised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> in 1757 that restricted all Western maritime trade to the single port of Guangzhou, known to Europeans as Canton. Foreign merchants had to trade through licensed Chinese merchant intermediaries known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohong">Cohong</a>. Western resentment of the system contributed to the tensions that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opium-wars">Opium Wars</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/canton-system.jpg" alt="Canton System" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canton_c1850.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
